  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Isolated to scattered showers expected tonight.\n\nA low/mid-level shortwave trough axis embedded in WNW'erly mean\nflow in the 925-500 mb layer will traverse our region late this\nevening through the first few predawn hours of Monday morning. \nMoist isentropic ascent ahead of the shortwave trough axis will \ntrigger isolated to scattered rain showers, mainly light in \nnature. These showers should exit our region generally from west\nto east between about midnight and 3 AM EDT Monday. At the \nsurface, a ridge builds slowly from the Upper Midwest through \ndaybreak. Behind the recent cold front passage, the low- level \nairmass may grow cold enough to support a brief period of \nscattered and light lake effect rain showers over and generally \nsoutheast of the eastern Great Lakes, mainly across the Genesee \nValley and western Finger Lakes, during the predawn hours of \nMonday morning. Any showers that develop are expected to end \naround daybreak as a much drier synoptic-scale airmass arrives, \nwhich combined with a lowering subsidence inversion accompanying\nthe building surface ridge, will cause already weak lake- \ninduced CAPE to wane. Lows are expected to reach the mid 40's to\nmid 50's around daybreak Monday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Brief cooldown Monday through early Tuesday morning, \nthen trending warmer and unsettled through the middle of the week.\n\nA much cooler airmass will briefly settle into the eastern Great \nLakes Monday, with 850MB temps bottoming out in the +4C to +6C \nrange. This will only support highs in the mid to upper 60s for \nlower elevations, and lower 60s for higher terrain. The cool \nnorthwest flow will support some lake effect and upslope clouds in \nthe morning, but these will scatter out later in the day as dry air \nand subsidence build into the region.\n\nSurface high pressure will settle over NY and western New England \nMonday night, with good radiational cooling conditions allowing for \nlows to drop back into the 40s areawide. A few of the normally \ncolder Southern Tier Valleys and Tug Hill region may make a run at \nthe upper 30s, but any patchy frost is likely to remain confined to \nthe coldest spots in the Adirondacks.\n\nThe high will move quickly east off the New England coast Tuesday, \nwith strong warm advection developing following a chilly start to \nthe day.\n\nA cold front will cross the Great Lakes Tuesday night through \nWednesday, bringing the next chance of showers. The front may stall \nand oscillate back and forth across the Ohio Valley and eastern \nGreat Lakes Wednesday night through Thursday night, supporting \nongoing chances of showers and a few thunderstorms.",
